      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dover was founded in 1818 and was named after Dover, England, the hometown of its founder Arthur Fox Jr.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A post office opened in Dover in 1833, marking the town’s early communication link.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Dover was officially incorporated in 1836, becoming a home rule-class city in Mason County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Situated on the south bank of the Ohio River, Dover faces Brown County, Ohio, across the water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dover%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Dover is part of the Maysville Micropolitan Statistical Area, linking it to regional economic activity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
